// SECURITY.md, which stays the enforcement record. en-only: a store listing
// needs one URL, and the store reviewer reads English.
const security = 'https://github.com/midagedev/gadak/blob/main/SECURITY.md'
const updated = '2026-09-03'
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  title="Privacy — gadak"
  description="gadak has no account, no server, and no telemetry. What the app stores on your machine, the six places it can send bytes, and what this website records."
  noAltLang
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    gadak is a local application. It mirrors an issue tracker you already have access to onto
    your own disk and hands that mirror to tools you run yourself. There is no gadak account, no
    gadak server, and no telemetry. Nothing you do in the app is reported to the maintainer.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    This page is the plain-language summary. The enforcement record — which file holds what, and
    the line of code behind each claim — is <a href={security}>SECURITY.md</a>. Where the two
    disagree, SECURITY.md is right. Last updated {updated}.
  </p>

  <h2 id="collect">What the maintainer collects</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing. The desktop app, the CLI, and the phone app send no usage data, crash reports,
    identifiers, or content to the maintainer or to any analytics service. The maintainer has no
    way to see that you installed gadak, which tracker you connected, or what it holds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="on-disk">What the app stores on your machine</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The mirror</strong> — a SQLite copy of the issues, comments, history, and wiki pages
      your tracker account can already read, plus cached attachment bytes on demand. It is a
      disposable cache: delete it and re-sync. Files are readable by your OS user only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your credential</strong> — the API token you typed at setup, in a config file with
      owner-only permissions. It is sent only as the authorization header to the site you
      configured, and is never written to the mirror, a log, or an error message.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Local history</strong> — which issues you opened and what you searched, kept in a
      separate local file so it survives deleting the mirror. It is yours to export or delete.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A log file</strong> — diagnostic output with token values stripped before writing.
      It never leaves the machine unless you paste it somewhere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The built-in tracker</strong> — if you use gadak without an external tracker, the
      issues you create live in one SQLite file in the workspace directory. That file is the only
      copy anywhere; there is no cloud sync.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Removing the gadak home directory removes all of the above. The macOS app additionally uses a
    system web view to show tracker pages the mirror does not model; that view's cookie session
    belongs to the operating system's web engine, and gadak neither reads nor stores it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="network">Where the app can send bytes</h2>
  <p>Outbound traffic goes to exactly these destinations, and to no others:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The tracker you configured</strong> — your own Atlassian site, or Linear — over
      HTTPS with your own token, to sync and to write back the comments, transitions, and edits
      you make. The tracker's own privacy policy governs what happens there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GitHub Releases</strong> — at most one anonymous request per day to check whether a
      newer version exists. It carries no identifier and no local data, and can be turned off in
      settings. Development builds never check.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A machine you paired with</strong> — if you bind a workspace to your own
      <code>gadak serve</code> on another computer (typically over your private network), requests
      go to that computer with the device token you minted there. That is your machine, not a
      gadak-operated server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Commands you invoke by hand</strong> — <code>gadak dev scan</code> runs the GitHub
      CLI you already have configured, and <code>gadak dashboards lib add</code> downloads the one
      URL you typed. Neither runs on a schedule.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The local web UI listens on loopback only. The desktop app opens no network port at all.
    Neither is reachable from other machines unless you configure it to be.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agents">Coding agents</h2>
  <p>
    gadak's purpose is to let an agent you run read your tracker's history. An agent that reads
    the mirror will send what it reads to whatever model it talks to; that is the agent's data
    flow, not gadak's, and it is the same exposure as pasting the issue into a chat window. If your
    organization would not allow that, do not point an agent at that mirror.
  </p>

  <h2 id="website">This website</h2>
  <p>
    gadak.dev's marketing pages — this one included — load Cloudflare Web Analytics, a cookieless
    page-view counter that stores nothing on your device and does not identify you across sites.
    The live demo and the public backlog apps carry no analytics beacon. The site sets one
    browser-storage key to remember your language choice, only after you pick one. Nothing here
    asks for an account or an email address.
  </p>

  <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
  <p>
    gadak is a developer tool with no account and collects nothing, so there is no data about
    anyone — of any age — for the maintainer to hold.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes and contact</h2>
  <p>
    This page changes when the data flow changes, and the date at the top moves with it. The
    project is maintained by one person; questions go to the
    <a href="https://github.com/midagedev/gadak/issues">issue tracker</a>, and anything sensitive
    to <a href="https://github.com/midagedev/gadak/security/advisories/new"
      >private vulnerability reporting</a
    >.
  </p>
</Base>
